== Affections of Many Kinds

VII. Persons having religious affections of many kinds, accompanying one another, is not enough to determine whether they have any gracious affections or not.

False religion is usually maimed and misshapen, and does not have the wholeness and symmetry of parts that is to be seen in true religion. Yet there may be a great variety of false affections together which may resemble gracious affections.

It is evident that there are counterfeits of all kinds of gracious affections. There are counterfeits of #emph[love to God] and #emph[love to the brethren,] as has just been observed. So there are of #emph[godly sorrow for sin,] as in #emph[Pharaoh, Saul] and #emph[Ahab,] and the children of #emph[Israel] in the wilderness (Exod 9:27; 1 Sam 24:16–17; 26:21; 1 Kgs 21:27; Num 14:39–40). And there are of the #emph[fear of God,] as in the #emph[Samaritans, who feared the LORD and served their own gods] at the same time (2 Kgs 17:32–33), and in those enemies of God we read of in Psalm 66:3, who #emph[through the greatness of God’s power submit themselves to him,] or, as it is in the Hebrew, #emph[lie to him,] that is, give him a counterfeit reverence and submission. So there are of a #emph[gracious gratitude,] as in the children of #emph[Israel] who sang God’s praise at the Red Sea (Ps 106:12), and in #emph[Naaman] the #emph[Syrian] after his miraculous cure of his leprosy (2 Kgs 5:15 and following).

So there are of #emph[spiritual joy,] as in the hearers on stony ground (Matt 13:20), and particularly in many of #emph[John the Baptist’s] hearers (John 5:35). So there are of #emph[zeal,] as in #emph[Jehu] (2 Kgs 10:16), and in #emph[Paul] before his conversion (Gal 1:14; Phil 3:6), and in the unbelieving #emph[Jews] (Acts 22:3; Rom 10:2). So persons without grace may have earnest religious #emph[desires,] which may be like #emph[Balaam’s] desires. He expressed them under an extraordinary view he had of the happy state of God’s people, as distinguished from all the rest of the world (Num 23:9–10). They may also have a strong #emph[hope] of eternal life, as the Pharisees had.

And as men in a state of nature are capable of a likeness of all kinds of religious affections, so nothing prevents their having many of them together. And what appears in fact abundantly proves that it is indeed very often so. It seems commonly to be the case that when false affections are raised high, many false affections accompany each other. The multitude that accompanied Christ into #emph[Jerusalem] after that great miracle of raising #emph[Lazarus] seem to have been moved with many religious affections at once, and all in a high degree. They seem to have been filled with #emph[wonder.] There was a show of a high affection of #emph[love,] and also of a great degree of #emph[reverence,] in their laying their garments on the ground for Christ to tread on. There was a show of great #emph[gratitude] to him for the great and good works he had done, as they praised him with loud voices for his salvation. There was a show of earnest #emph[desires] for the coming of God’s kingdom, which they supposed #emph[Jesus] was now about to set up, and they showed great #emph[hopes] and raised expectations of it, #emph[expecting it to appear immediately.] So they were filled with #emph[joy,] by which they were so animated in their acclamations as to make the whole city ring with the noise of them. And they appeared great in their #emph[zeal] and eagerness to accompany Jesus and help him set up his kingdom without further delay, now at the time of the great feast of the #emph[Passover.] And it is easy, from nature and the nature of the affections, to account for why one affection, when raised very high, should stir up others, especially if the affection raised high is counterfeit #emph[love,] as it was in the multitude who cried #emph[Hosanna.] This will naturally draw many other affections after it. For, as was observed before, love is the chief of the affections, and as it were the fountain of them. Let us suppose a person who has been for some time in great turmoil of soul and terror through fear of hell, his heart weakened with distress and dreadful apprehensions, and on the brink of despair. And suppose he is all at once delivered, by being made to believe firmly, through some delusion of #emph[Satan,] that God has pardoned him, accepts him as the object of his dear love, and promises him eternal life. Suppose this comes through some vision, or strong idea or imagination suddenly stirred up in him, of a person with a beautiful face, smiling on him, with arms open and with blood dropping down, which the person takes to be Christ, without any other enlightening of the understanding to give a view of the spiritual, divine excellence of Christ and his fullness, and of the way of salvation revealed in the gospel. Or perhaps it comes by some voice or words coming as if they were spoken to him, such as, “Take courage, son; your sins are forgiven,” or, “Do not be afraid … it is your#super[+] Father’s good pleasure to give you#super[+] the kingdom,” which he takes to be spoken to him immediately by God, though there was no previous acceptance of Christ, or the heart’s embrace of him. I say, if we suppose such a case, what various passions would naturally crowd into such a person’s mind, all at once or one after another? It is easy to account, from mere principles of nature, for such a person’s heart being raised to the skies on such an occasion with transports of joy. It is easy to account for his being filled with fervent affection to that imaginary God or Redeemer who he supposes has rescued him in this way from the jaws of such dreadful destruction, the fears of which so overwhelmed his soul, and has received him with such endearment as a special favorite. It is easy to account for his now being filled with wonder and gratitude, and his mouth being opened and full of talk about what he has experienced; and for his thinking and speaking of hardly anything else for a while, seeming to magnify the God who has done so much for him, calling on others to rejoice with him, appearing with a cheerful face and talking with a loud voice. However full of quarreling against the justice of God he was before his deliverance, it is easy to account for its now being easy for him to submit to God, acknowledge his unworthiness, cry out against himself, appear very humble before God, and lie at his feet as tame as a lamb. And it is easy to account for his now confessing his unworthiness and crying out, #emph[“Why me? Why me?”] (Like #emph[Saul,] who, when #emph[Samuel] told him that God had appointed him to be king, answered, “Am I not a Benjamite from the smallest tribe of Israel, and is not my clan the least of all the clans of Benjamin? So why would you say such a thing to me?” This is much in the language of #emph[David,] the true saint, in 2 Sam 7:18: “Who am I, O Lord GOD, and what is my house, that You have brought me this far?”) Nor is it any wonder that he should now delight to be with those who acknowledge and applaud his happy circumstances, and should love all such as esteem and admire him and what he has experienced. Nor is it any wonder that he should have violent zeal against all such as would make nothing of such things, and be inclined to separate openly, and as it were to proclaim war on all who are not of his party. Nor is it any wonder that he should now glory in his sufferings, and be very ready to condemn and censure all who seem to doubt these things or raise any difficulty about them; and that while the warmth of his affections lasts, he should be mightily eager to take pains and deny himself to promote the interest of the party which he imagines favors such things, and should seem earnestly desirous to increase their number, as the #emph[Pharisees] traveled over sea and land to make one #emph[proselyte]#footnote[Associating with godly men does not prove that a man has grace: #emph[Ahithophel] was #emph[David’s] companion. Sorrow for the afflictions of the church, and desires for the conversion of souls, do not prove it. These things may be found in carnal men, and so can be no evidences of grace. Stoddard’s #emph[nature of saving conversion,] p. 82.]. And so I might go on and mention many other things that will naturally arise in such circumstances. A man must have considered human nature only slightly if he thinks such things as these cannot arise in this way without any supernatural intervention of divine power.

As all Christian affections flow from true divine love, so in the same way other false affections naturally flow from a counterfeit love. In both cases love is the fountain, and the other affections are the streams. The various faculties, principles and affections of human nature are like many channels from one fountain. If there is sweet water in the fountain, sweet water will flow out from it into those various channels. But if the water in the fountain is poisonous, then poisonous streams will also flow out into all those channels. So the channels and streams will be alike, corresponding to one another, but the great difference will lie in the nature of the water. Or man’s nature may be compared to a tree with many branches coming from one root. If the sap in the root is good, good sap will also be distributed throughout the branches, and the fruit that is brought forth will be good and wholesome. But if the sap in the root and trunk is poisonous, so it will be in many branches (as in the other case), and the fruit will be deadly. The tree in both cases may be alike; there may be an exact resemblance in shape. But the difference is found only in eating the fruit. It is often like this, in some measure at least, between saints and hypocrites. There is at times a very great likeness between true and false experiences in their appearance, and in what is expressed and related by those who have them. And the difference between them is much like the difference between the dreams of #emph[Pharaoh’s] chief cupbearer and baker. They seemed to be much alike, so much so that when #emph[Joseph] interpreted the chief cupbearer’s dream, that he would be delivered from his imprisonment and restored to the king’s favor and his honorable office in the palace, the chief baker’s hopes and expectations were raised, and he told his dream also. But he was woefully disappointed. And though his dream was so much like the happy and hopeful dream of his companion, yet it was quite contrary in its outcome.
